1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:31,440
The saved school index contains eleven Week 14 rows: a broad A&P
review, BCEHS A01 through A04, BCPEG practical examination criteria,
and assignments in CV Patho, CNS, CPSC, FON, MSN, pharmacology,
mental-health nursing and vital-sign measurement. The source identity
and exact assigned-range status are stated in each reading, rather
than treating every recovered file as an unverified candidate.

2
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:45,744
Nancy is the learner-confirmed main examination textbook. Its selected
EENT and clinical-decision sections are an additional study bridge,
not a twelfth school reading row and not a translation of the assigned
chapters.

3
00:00:45,744 --> 00:01:10,560
The assignment table identifies the reading requirement; the actual
school-folder copy identifies the text that can be checked. Several
repeated safety and communication ranges now have verified source
identities and earlier full-range explanations. A similar title alone
is still insufficient, and a source or page difference remains
explicit rather than being silently cleared.

4
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:35,808
This workshop uses original paired English and Chinese teaching and
retains the exact school locator beside the actual mapping. It does
not reproduce source patient stories, figures, assessment questions or
answer keys. Review status is tied to the source and teaching
evidence, not the number of files, slides or links. Remaining reading
gaps are not presented as completed.

5
00:01:35,808 --> 00:01:53,880
For the child airway case, connect respiratory anatomy, pediatric
physiology and danger cues; for eye exposure, connect chemical
contact, infection-control/PPE and rapid irrigation; for bleeding,
connect risk history with a simple first-aid action.

6
00:01:53,880 --> 00:02:08,040
Do not use nursing texts or broad physiology readings as field
authorization. Clinical pathways, provincial legislation, current
service policy, licensing and supervised instruction define the
operative boundary.

7
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:21,912
For each answer, state the source you used and whether it is a school
objective, current BCEHS direction or a candidate-text concept. That
habit prevents a textbook summary from being mistaken for a current
treatment order.

8
00:02:21,912 --> 00:02:35,808
When studying for a test, distinguish recall from application. Recall
the school’s stated criteria; then apply the current source and
explain how you would respond if equipment, patient condition or scene
safety changed.

9
00:02:35,808 --> 00:02:54,600
The school plan requests a Sunday self-assessment using Appendix G and
three weak points for Week 16 remediation. The official appendix was
not present in the available source set. Use the linked original
three-gap method only as preparation; do not submit it in place of the
school form.

10
00:02:54,600 --> 00:03:13,392
A useful gap says what happened, when it happened, why it matters and
how the next run will test a different behavior. For example: “I
waited for saline while the eye remained exposed; next run I will
start suitable clean water while my partner identifies the product.”
